// User-layer example 2 — notice a usage-limit banner that only the screen shows.
// Teaches: `facts.screen.*`, `field_regex` on the screen's text, and why a
// screen fact never appears in a reason.
//
// A provider's usage-limit notice is drawn on the terminal and often reaches no
// transcript Karen can read. For a session started as `karen run <runtime>`,
// the terminal host holds the screen, and a gate can ask about it:
//
//   facts.screen.usage_limit  true when a limit banner is among the last rows
//   facts.screen.text         the whole visible screen, for field_regex only
//
// This gate uses the text so the pattern is yours to tune. Two rules to know:
//
// * The screen is the conversation as it stands, so it can hold anything. It
//   is matched and never shown: `{facts.screen.text}` in a reason stays
//   literal, nothing writes it to a log, nothing caches it. The reason below
//   is therefore fixed text.
// * A session that is not hosted by `karen run` for this runtime has no screen
//   facts at all, and this gate stays quiet (absence is never a match).
//
// Provisional: the pattern follows how the runtimes word their limit notices
// and has not been checked against a banner recorded from one. Look at what
// your runtime really prints, and adjust. Ships disabled for that reason.
{
  "id": "screen-shows-a-usage-limit",
  "name": "A usage limit is on the screen",
  "description": "Notify when the hosted session's screen shows a usage or rate limit banner.",
  "event": "Stop",
  "priority": 50,
  "enabled": false,
  "tags": ["facts", "screen", "notify"],

  "match": {
    "all": [
      { "type": "field_regex", "field": "facts.screen.text", "pattern": "(?i)(usage|rate|session|weekly) limit" }
    ]
  },

  "action": {
    "type": "notify",
    "reason": "The session's screen shows a usage limit. Check the terminal before the next prompt."
  }
}
